<h2>A partition suit grows a new prayer</h2>
<p>The plaintiff sued in May 2014 for partition and injunctions over two Karawal Nagar properties acquired by the parties&rsquo; late father — property A-1302 (300 sq. yds.) and House No. 91 (about 200 sq. yds.). In his written statement of October 2014, the first defendant disclosed that 75 sq. yds. of A-1302 had been transferred to the second defendant by documents dated 25 March 1998. The plaintiff denied this in his replication of February 2015. In February 2019 he sought to amend the plaint — to implead the transferee as a defendant and to add a declaration that the 1998 documents are null and void. By order of 11 January 2024, the Additional District Judge allowed the amendment subject to costs of Rs 4,000. The defendants petitioned under Article 227.</p>

<h2>The competing cases</h2>
<p>The petitioners argued that the new declaratory relief was barred by Article 58 of the Limitation Act, because the plaintiff knew of the transfer long before 2019 — they pointed to an action taken report of April 2012, an order of June 2012 and a complaint of April 2014 — and that the trial court had deferred the limitation question to evidence instead of deciding it on the record. They also contended the amendment changed the nature of a partition suit, relying among others on <em>S.P. Chengalvaraya Naidu v. Jagannath</em>, <em>Life Insurance Corporation v. Sanjeev Builders</em> and <em>Basavaraj v. Indira</em>. The respondent answered that he learned of the transfer only from the written statement, disputed the genuineness of the 1998 documents, denied delivery of possession, and maintained the claim was within time.</p>

<h2>What Article 227 does — and what the trial court did not do</h2>
<p>Justice Digpaul restated the discipline of supervisory jurisdiction: it is exercised sparingly, does not reappreciate evidence, and intervenes only for a patent error or manifest failure of justice. The question was whether the trial court had applied the Order VI Rule 17 parameters. Since trial had not commenced, the due-diligence proviso was not the obstacle. The governing framework came from <em>Life Insurance Corporation v. Sanjeev Builders</em> — including the principle that a time-barred claim is a relevant factor against amendment — from the factors catalogued in <em>Revajeetu Builders v. Narayanaswamy</em>, and from <em>Pankaja v. Yellappa</em> on amendments seeking declarations of title.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Objection raised.</strong> The defendants squarely pleaded that the declaratory claim was barred under Article 58.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Objection noticed, then parked.</strong> The trial court deferred limitation to evidence without examining the documents relied upon or recording a reasoned conclusion.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Second gap.</strong> The order did not address whether the new declaration changed the nature of the partition suit.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Result.</strong> An order that, in the High Court&rsquo;s words, did not reflect a consideration of the rival contentions on the governing parameters.</div>
</div>
<p>The Court held that limitation cannot be dealt with mechanically at the amendment stage: it must be considered then, on the material placed before the court. That the amendment concerned the same property was relevant, but not decisive, on whether the suit&rsquo;s nature changed. Pointedly, the Court declined to express any view of its own on limitation, the 1998 documents or the character of the suit.</p>

<h2>The order and its practical meaning</h2>
<p>The petition was allowed, the order of 11 January 2024 set aside, and the amendment application restored to be decided afresh by a reasoned order after hearing both sides — preferably within four weeks — with all contentions left open.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p>For plaintiffs: an amendment adding a declaratory challenge to old documents must be supported on limitation at the threshold. Plead with precision when knowledge was acquired, and meet the opponent&rsquo;s documents head-on.</p>
  <p>For defendants: a limitation objection to an amendment is not an objection for trial; insist on a finding, and an order that defers it without reasons is vulnerable under Article 227.</p>
  <p>For both: Article 227 corrects method, not merits. The High Court sent the question back; it did not answer it. The battle over Article 58 will now be fought where it should have been fought first.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article describes a supervisory judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information. The amendment application it concerns remains pending decision afresh before the trial court.</div>
